{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"3490b473d7b2ab5d422c4e2776e61fee3c04149f8299247a7e567ad18df06f5b","slug":"compilers-basic","location":"/src/1-Fundamentals/200-Building_code/1-compilers_BASIC.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"The language of the CPU","content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":3}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"1-compilers_BASIC.md","url":"/daje_website/build/1-compilers_BASIC-e6b5e7e8cd275ef7f2ea1818e0648308.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"QVpxv9UX1u"}],"key":"X7jtdmeL0z"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A CPU does not execute C, Fortran, or Python directly. It executes very simple instructions such as “copy this value”, “add these two numbers”, “compare two values”, or “jump to another instruction.”","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"VSCjQEeuEA"}],"key":"Cr9PiFsK99"}],"kind":"note","class":"simple","key":"P7eGoErf6N"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Machine language","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"mIrRkMRvLY"}],"identifier":"machine-language","label":"Machine language","html_id":"machine-language","implicit":true,"key":"GGTuHy723t"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the heart of every computer system lies machine language, the most basic form of code that the CPU can execute directly. Machine language consists of binary digits, that is, sequences of 0s and 1s. These binary instructions directly control the operations performed by the CPU.","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"t0iFuMV7pA"}],"key":"kkwZugDTEf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writing programs directly in machine language is extremely difficult for humans. The problem is not only that machine language is written in binary form. The real difficulty is that the programmer must manually specify every low-level operation: which instruction to execute, which registers to use, where data are stored, and how the program moves from one instruction to the next.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"aU9dYJNm1r"}],"key":"aMxWeYCoqv"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory and registers","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"fGaKd0Qo2K"}],"identifier":"memory-and-registers","label":"Memory and registers","html_id":"memory-and-registers","implicit":true,"key":"rVR82ZMYVj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To understand even a very simple machine instruction, we need two basic ideas: memory and registers.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"rSXNKI2D52"}],"key":"kOfDl2O4zS"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory stores the data used by a program. It can be imagined as a long sequence of cells, each identified by an address. Variables in high-level languages, such as C or Fortran, correspond to data stored somewhere in memory.","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"bJhEAl9wCt"}],"key":"ScIvymkSH0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A register is a very small and very fast storage location inside the CPU","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"fXqGhp4fFT"}],"key":"p8wwtekWyZ"},{"type":"text","value":". Registers are used to temporarily hold values while the CPU executes instructions.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"hN0cMhFLOs"}],"key":"D5CNzNdu6S"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, consider the following C instruction:","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"tM94YMcq8N"}],"key":"CUzW9cLIRm"},{"type":"code","lang":"C","value":"x = x + 10; ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"dgF4dTMRDJ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the CPU level, this operation may correspond conceptually to something like:","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"z3mReTu2Zk"}],"key":"DcHSBzYI0w"},{"type":"code","lang":"assembly","value":"MOV AX, x     ; copy the value of x into the AX register \nADD AX, 10    ; add 10 to the value stored in AX \nMOV x, AX     ; copy the result back into memory ","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"Gv74XxGYBX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is not meant to be exact assembly code. The important idea is the following: memory → register → operation → memory","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"